concept("airflow-deep", "de", "Airflow in depth: sensors, XCom, catchup and pools", OP, "I",
  "airflow dag operator task sensor xcom catchup backfill pool retries sla scheduler logical date execution date deferrable taskflow",
  "Beyond basic DAGs, Airflow has features for waiting on external events, passing small values between tasks, limiting concurrency and rerunning history.",
  "Operators define what each task does. Sensors wait for a condition such as a file arriving or another DAG finishing, and deferrable sensors wait without holding a worker slot. XCom passes small values, like a file path or a row count, between tasks; large data belongs in storage, not XCom. Each run's logical date tells it which data interval it covers, so with catchup enabled Airflow schedules every missed interval, and a backfill reruns any past range. Pools and max_active_runs limit concurrency to protect databases and APIs. Retries, timeouts, SLAs and callbacks handle failure and alerting.",
  "A daily DAG waits with a deferrable S3 sensor for the partner file, loads it, pushes the row count through XCom to a validation task, and runs in a pool limited to 4 slots so it never overloads the source database.",
  "Why shouldn't a task pass a 500 MB DataFrame to the next task through XCom?",
  "Expect sensors, XCom limits, catchup and backfill, pools, and how you make DAGs idempotent and easy to debug.",
  ["orchestration", "backfills", "idempotency"]),
